When margins collapse through every in-flow child of a block box that
isn’t a formatting context root, and the collapsed margin can collapse
with neither the box’s top margin (padding or a border above it) nor
its bottom margin (padding or a border below it), Ladybird makes the
box run to the bottom of that margin, from its top content edge, as
section 10.6.3 of CSS 2 says. Chrome 158, Firefox 151 and WebKit 26.5
agree on all 13 cases in the test: padding or borders at both ends,
both margins of the child, two children, mixed signs, a negative margin
(clamped to zero), a child nested a level deeper, padding or a border
at one end only, a box before the empty one, and flow roots.

Blink adds the end margin strut’s Sum() to the block size in
BlockLayoutAlgorithm where the block end is separated by padding or a
border. Gecko’s nsBlockFrame::ComputeFinalSize() doesn’t carry the
children’s block-end margin out of a box that is a block-end margin
root, but takes it into its height. And WebKit’s RenderBlockFlow::
handleAfterSideOfBlock() adds the margin its MarginInfo still holds
where it can’t collapse with the margin after the box.
